WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Q-Bert, this game is so mind-boggling, it will knock you out.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:27.260
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.420
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.780
And that's it.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.960
It's another one of the Fireside Chats, mostly always about arcade games.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.820
I don't know what that is, Sean, but sometimes the other guys just don't want to join us.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:43.700
It's totally, I'm sure it's totally a coincidence that it's about the games that they've all

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.220
played a million times before.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:51.480
It's fine. And Q-Bert could be one of those games that some people have played a million times before.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.480
Remind me, what's your level of familiarity with our little orange friend?

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:00.360
Going to those bar-arcade combination places.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.160
I played them there.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:08.500
And at an arcade called Kahunaville in a mall.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.420
That's where I played Q-Bert.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:17.500
I first played Q-Bert online, actually, back in like 2001 or 2002.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:23.900
I guess somebody found a way to like emulate those games online, like just with Java or

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:28.080
something like that. So I'm not certain if it was even the real arcade version or what have you.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.900
But I don't know.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:35.500
It was fun. It was like just simple enough that you wanted to keep playing.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.720
Not as complicated as some of the other arcade games that were hosted on that website.

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.500
So it was one that I frequently went back to.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.740
But it's also not something that we hear much about today.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.180
I don't think anyone's screaming for Q-Bert's revival.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:56.540
Yeah. I think for me, it was always like it had a unique enough look because of the unique angle

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:57.680
that it presents it at.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:04.220
And again, like you said, it was simple enough that I was easily drawn to it.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:11.900
And then when I would eventually just fail, I was just like, OK, I'm just not good at that.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.700
And then I'd walk away and play something else.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.960
You know, people in 1983, though, when this game came out, they were what I would say Q-Bert

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.240
crazy for Q-Bert.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.280
You know, they were crazy for Q-Bert.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.420
God, stop it.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:31.440
And I say that not even just because the arcade game was a hit, but because we're talking about

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:37.700
over 125 licensed merchandise based on Q-Bert's likeliness.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.360
Everything from Frisbees to sleeping bags to bedsheets.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.000
I mean, you name it, they made it for Q-Bert.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:46.160
For Q-Bert.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:51.620
Wow. That's some really uncontrolled consumerism.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:53.720
I like where you're going with this.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.080
Thank you for following along.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:01.620
You know, Q-Bert also even got an animated cartoon as part of Saturday Supercade on CBS.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.680
So I don't know if you managed to like find stumble of this on like YouTube or something,

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:07.920
but the episodes are up there.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:13.260
And I just think it's hysterical, you know, that you could flesh out something like Q-Bert

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:18.780
who kind of lives in a void in space, you know, like just on a floating pyramid.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:24.760
Yeah. So I guess then you'd be qualified because I imagine you watched the entire show.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:30.680
So maybe you're qualified to answer this question because I know that you're really into baseball.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:34.940
But my question is, is Q-Bert just the Philly fanatic?

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:42.280
You know, best I can tell, Q-Bert, you know, the Q-Bert that we know in the video games

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:46.500
is not the same Q-Bert that they put into this television show.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.360
So in the television show, right, because like in the television show, he's like,

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.120
it's almost like it's 1950 or something.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.100
Like there's just, there's something off about Q-Bert's world.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:59.780
And he's got a girlfriend, by the way, named QT.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.240
Oh, wow. You know, so he also has a brother, Q-Bit.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:06.540
You know, I could just, you know, everybody just Q something.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.960
And they basically deal with, you know, the same people that you deal with in the game,

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:19.120
like Coy Lee and Ugg and Wrong Way, which is funny to think that like that's the name of the enemies.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.340
But, you know, they're basically just dealing with them, but in more like social settings.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.700
Like those guys are bullies to the townspeople.

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.920
And Q-Bert's kind of like, you know, that's not cool.

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.620
Yeah. I mean, you kind of ignored my question now.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:37.120
No. Oh, yeah.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.520
So about the Philly Fanatic thing.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:44.200
No, he's, I guess like what I'm saying is like I would imagine he is if you go off of the arcade game.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:50.900
But I just don't buy that the same Q-Bert that's on this TV show, you know, who talks.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.360
First off, that's like the weirdest thing.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.880
Yeah. Like they should have not have said a word.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:05:01.160
But no, he seems to be pretty level-headed and, you know, supposed to be our hero.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:09.020
I'd say that like anything like that, this Q-Bert show is more just Q-Bert Gaiden than anything.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.660
I don't think this is true deep lore in the TV show.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.000
Whereas like the Philly Fanatic, he's like a foil.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.760
He's a court jester, if you will.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.260
So much more like a Q-Bert video game.

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:22.720
Yes, exactly.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:35.700
Exactly. If I had it my way, and I think both Wreck-It Ralph and that other movie Pixels with Adam Sandler, both of them have Q-Bert and he doesn't say a damn word in either one.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.780
And that's how I would choose to portray Q-Bert as well.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:47.880
It's just kind of like this goofy, otherworldly thing that like you recognize, but you're like, oh, that's pretty strange that, you know, he's forced to be alive.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:50.580
Against his will.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:51.680
Right, right.

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:57.460
I brought all this up in the front though, just to say that like, you know, Q-Bert was a big deal.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:02.060
And then like the video game crash happened, you know, like the same exact year.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:05.160
So the popularity of the character pretty much declined in 1984.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:14.220
But, you know, that doesn't necessarily explain a lot of things because like Pac-Man and Donkey Kong, Donkey Kong is still popular because he got like attached to the Mario series.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:16.380
But like Pac-Man, still very popular.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:22.560
Why isn't Q-Bert like something that, you know, like we're not looking for more of that?

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.760
I guess we're going to have to start some petitions.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.680
Well, I'll start with, you know, just some observations of my own.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:31.300
You know, I'm nitpicky about this stuff.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:36.180
The title, Q-Bert, I don't know, it bothers me because we haven't been saying it properly.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:38.640
It's like, it's Q asterisk Bert.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:47.260
Yeah, I mean, would you, like if it was hyphenated, would you think that you have to say it any differently?

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:54.860
No, but I think that's like the point of the hyphen, right, is to let me know that it's like, it's Q-Bert is like one word.

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.700
Whereas like the asterisk doesn't tell me much about anything.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.500
Is the Q silent by the asterisk?

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:03.380
Like, is that like a footnote that you have to read further down?

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:05.660
Oh, God. Yeah, you're right.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.300
You're right. I think it's supposed to be Q to the power of Bert.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:13.840
That would also, that would work for me a little bit better.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:15.680
You know, like that would be fun to say every time.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:20.460
Q to the power. Also, just general musings here.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:29.040
Because Q-Bert, when he, you know, like when he gets hit by something, he kind of like curses in random symbols, right?

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:35.060
He has like the at sign and the exclamation mark and maybe a few other, maybe the ampersand shows up.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:37.000
He has like these curses that he does.

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:41.260
So that made me think of like, you know, why isn't it Q-Wert?

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:47.900
Like the first, you know, four letters on an actual keyboard, like a QWERTY keyboard, like Q-Wert.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:54.660
And it's like when he dies, he's just like rage quitting and spamming a bunch of symbols on the keyboard.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:56.920
You know, that like Q-Wert makes sense to me.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:58.700
Q-Bert? I'm like, I don't really know this guy.

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:05.900
Maybe the idea was that it would be like, again, we're going into territory where nobody's an expert on anything.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:10.960
But I would say that maybe that was the original idea.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:16.000
But to make it sound more namey, they made it Q-Bert.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:21.060
And that you're supposed to just understand that it's based on the keyboard, you know?

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:22.980
Or it could be nothing.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:27.380
And we're just, we're reading into things that you don't need to.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:38.380
Warren Davis, who is the creator of Q-Bert, did express regret for using the atterisk because it prevented the name from being a common crossword term.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.800
Oh, that's, wow.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:42.620
Okay, that's the sort of things he's thinking about.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.520
It could have lived on for a hundred years.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:48.780
He just removed the asterisk.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:50.340
He's actually responsible.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.440
The whole thing that we were asking earlier about, why did Q-Bert die?

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:54.940
Why is no one asking for Q-Bert?

00:08:55.080 --> 00:09:01.920
It's because there's this uncertainty around the asterisk that like no lawyer in America could answer.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.080
So they won't dare license the property.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:05.300
Wow. Yeah, I'm looking.

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:07.060
I've never noticed it before.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:15.080
But like just in like YouTube videos and just general Google results, nothing is, like everything has the asterisk.

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:20.480
I thought it was just like one of those stylizations that aren't always respected.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.440
But here we are.

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:28.420
Yeah, and I think like, you know, there's Q-Bert and then there's Q-Bert.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:30.740
So that's why the asterisk has to be there.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:33.240
You know, that's the emphasis on the Q, if you will.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.180
So, Sean, what are we playing here?

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:39.620
What is a Q-Bert?

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:51.680
A Q-Bert? I'm confident that a Q-Bert is a game that we're about to talk about, the game itself and not the weird alien creature.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:58.900
It's sort of like, I forgot what game we were talking about.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.840
Oh, no, we were talking about Pac-Man just a few episodes ago.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:07.100
We were discussing the feeling of playing Pac-Man.

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:12.600
And one of you said that it was like mowing the grass.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:22.180
And this is sort of just mowing the grass on a very cube-y hill, if that's a good enough way to describe it.

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:30.740
Yeah, it's like a bunch of actual cubes stacked on top of each other in the shape of a pyramid, I guess, is like the best way to describe that thing.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:38.060
And you have to touch the top layer of each cube to change its color.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:41.740
And this is all done in an isometric viewpoint.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.540
So, you know, the cubes do look 3D.

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:51.400
And Q-Bert even has a little bit of like 3D sense to him, right, in the way that a 2D video game could.

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:52.400
He's got some shading.

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:59.540
Yeah. So, you know, there's supposed to be this added challenge of it's not just like hopscotch.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:10.260
You know, it's like you actually have to – your inputs kind of are the important thing because you have to know where you are in space, you know, like at any given time.

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:18.760
Because if you hit to go right, well, what does right mean contextually when you're at the edge of the pyramid?

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:22.520
Whereas if you're in the middle of the pyramid, you know, so those things kind of change.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:24.920
And that's really like – that's the challenge, right?

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:29.200
The isometric nature of the game is the challenging part of the game.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:35.560
It was so challenging, in fact, that the – two things happen here.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:43.500
The manual suggests that to make the game easier, you should play with the controller at a 45-degree angle.

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:46.700
And when I read that, I had no idea what they meant.

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:55.140
I mean, I knew that the directions were not going to be just up, down, left, right because of this like faux 3D effect.

00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:02.760
But I didn't think they'd go so far as to like just pretend that your controller is these inputs instead.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:14.140
And then when you actually get into the game, it doesn't start off with the game itself, but you actually like customize your controls with a little helper dude.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:21.880
So I found those very interesting, both of those things going on here.

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:26.660
The helper dude especially though, I know that's not like the – it's a menu screen.

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:29.320
But like there is supposed – they show you a controller.

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:30.540
They have a guy there.

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:32.320
Like there's something –

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.780
Yeah, there's something great about that though.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.980
Like this could have just been – put in an options menu.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:45.800
Yeah. And just like asked you which way – you know, like it would just say up and then it would say, you know, up on the D-pad as well.

00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:52.140
Like it would be weird if there wasn't all of this extra visual aid to show you exactly what they were talking about.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:58.840
They actually have it broken down for you so that you can see like Hubert is on this square and he's going to jump to this square.

00:12:59.120 --> 00:13:01.760
What D-pad button do you think he should press?

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:04.540
And, you know, you kind of have to cycle through them and do some work.

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:10.220
But I think it's funny that the game lets you customize as far as like just even making it opposite.

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:12.980
Like you could say like, oh, Hubert's jumping upright.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:16.580
Well, I'm going to hit the left button on the D-pad for that.

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:18.780
You know, like you could totally screw with yourself too.

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:23.580
But I like that it allows full customization of how you control Hubert.

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:28.500
And not just like a mode A, mode B of how they might think people would use it.

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:30.340
I appreciate that too.

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:37.780
Yeah, and this is made by Ultra Games as well who were the same people that brought us Gyrus last episode.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:41.640
So that's interesting to think that like first they had the control A, control B option.

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:45.580
And then in this one they're like, you know, just give them full customization.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:50.860
And full customization is important because it's not just choose your direction on the D-pad.

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:54.100
They did the thing I was kind of hoping they would do.

00:13:54.100 --> 00:14:03.240
And my preferred method of playing this game on a D-pad controller instead of a joystick is they let you say up and to the right.

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:05.220
Like to have to press down both buttons.

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:06.860
Oh, you can do that?

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:08.640
Yeah. Oh, man.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:18.340
You can actually say like, yeah, for each thing, each corner I actually want to press up and to the right or right and down or right down and left.

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:20.320
You know, you can actually put that in there.

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:22.640
So this way you have to confirm both buttons.

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:26.460
But it actually makes it make a little more sense because you can picture.

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:30.180
Yeah, so it's more one-to-one instead of pretending that you're playing at an angle.

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:31.180
Right, right.

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:37.100
You can picture which cube you're going to jump on by that little like square hole that's left on the D-pad.

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:38.680
Yeah. Huh.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:44.940
That's really – that's like the first accessibility consideration I think we've seen.

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:50.000
Even though it's not like – it's not in the same sense as how you might think.

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.020
But I appreciated it.

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:56.280
Yeah, and even the – yeah, the 45-degree angle thing, though.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:01.680
Like I'm holding my NES controller right now and I'm thinking about that and I'm like, okay, maybe.

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:03.760
Like I don't – I have to actually play the game.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:05.740
But that seems a little more confusing.

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:08.140
It didn't work. Than just choosing whatever feels comfortable.

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:18.140
Because I did – playing the game, I did have perspective issues that I'll go into further when we get into it.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:22.300
But I did try to play it at that 45-degree angle that they were talking about.

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:25.060
And I guess my brain just doesn't work that way.

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:27.140
I just felt like I was holding the controller weird.

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:31.520
So, yeah, that was a weird suggestion for them.

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:38.380
Now, Sean, you mentioned about the mowing the lawn thing and Pac-Man.

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:43.440
That all happened because we started talking about the term like is it satisfying?

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:46.620
Yes. Arcade games are satisfying, right?

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:50.380
That's what makes those short loops worth it and why you keep putting quarters in.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:54.120
There's no grand prize at the end.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:57.160
It's all about just the reward of playing the game itself.

00:15:57.160 --> 00:15:58.960
And so I have to ask you here.

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:09.580
Since Q-Bert is kind of like a version of the floor is lava, is that satisfying to you of like touch the tiles and watch the colors change?

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:11.960
And you have to avoid some enemies.

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:20.500
But for the most part, you're just trying to change all of the colors of the tiles from like blue to yellow, yellow to green, green to red.

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:25.980
Like what did you think? I'd say that at the most basic.

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:27.760
So level one, round one.

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:36.020
I found it satisfying in that very basic way of like I'm just going to turn all these blue.

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:38.100
And that did feel good.

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:51.000
But the enemies in this game end up turning it for me mostly into like an exercise in frustration and kind of futility.

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:58.500
Especially the – I forgot their names but the ones that will just come and undo your progress.

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:03.360
That was just a little – that was like a bridge too far for me.

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:10.380
Maybe if they just didn't have the enemies that undid your progress, I could enjoy this a bit more.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:23.940
But unlike Pac-Man where there's no – like none of the ghosts are going to add more stuff for you to have to get rid of or like lay traps for you aside from them just existing.

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:31.280
I'd say that this wasn't as – this wasn't as enjoyable.

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:40.940
First, I just want to comment that I hope that the developers of Pac-Man are listening to this episode because that is absolutely something that needs to be added into the next iteration of Pac-Man.

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:45.180
Ghosts should be able to add traps and other crazy things.

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:47.140
Like they should have their own power-ups too.

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:51.000
I hope that that exists on some kind of level because that's a great idea.

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:52.500
You're right though.

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:56.560
It's a bit much here in Q-Bert at the very start.

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:57.640
I don't know about you.

00:17:58.040 --> 00:17:59.880
Level one was harder than level two.

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:03.240
And that shouldn't necessarily be how any game is run.

00:18:03.380 --> 00:18:04.480
Like I can't think of another example.

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:09.000
Usually level one, you're like all powered out or you're supposed to just like learn the ropes.

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:11.300
And this is just my own experience.

00:18:11.300 --> 00:18:16.460
But I found that stage one took me so many tries to actually like not die on.

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:18.220
And, you know, lives are a precious thing.

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:23.700
So you can't – you know, you can't really accept too many deaths on stage one and think you're going to get very far.

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:25.220
So I would just reset a lot.

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:32.840
But when I finally got to stage two, maybe I just got better at the game as a consequence of stage one being so hard.

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:34.900
But I found stage two to be a little more manageable.

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:41.640
I don't think it was the right call to say here's most of the game right off the bat in stage one.

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:43.940
And we're going to litter the screen with these guys too.

00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:46.820
Like Snake Boy is coming directly for you.

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:51.540
And the bouncing balls is like somebody was juggling and dropped all of them at once.

00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:53.640
Yeah, that's another thing.

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:55.600
Like the bouncing balls.

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:57.700
Like I don't consider them an enemy.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:18:59.160
I consider them an obstacle.

00:18:59.420 --> 00:19:05.980
And when it comes to obstacles, I feel like there's an unspoken rule that they should be predictable.

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:11.560
Like you can kind of – I mean a lot of great games break the rule.

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:20.580
Like in Donkey Kong, you'll – it's kind of random where the barrels will fall.

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:23.880
But you can still kind of tell what they're going to do.

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:26.740
You can guess more times than not.

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:32.580
But with the bouncing balls, I had no idea if they're going to fall down to the left, down to the right.

00:19:33.300 --> 00:19:36.000
And I feel like – I think they might be following you.

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:40.500
And they might just be a – like actually just an enemy.

00:19:42.400 --> 00:19:44.560
So that kind of pissed me off.

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:49.140
The fact that an obstacle was behaving like an enemy.

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:54.860
And it just – there's just so much going on, especially with the snakes.

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:57.640
And now I'm just – now I'm just ranting.

00:19:57.660 --> 00:19:58.820
So please stop me.

00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:03.900
Well, you're having a post-war flashback is what you're having.

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:12.180
Because I think you can watch somebody play this game and say to yourself like, oh, seems pretty straightforward.

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:13.140
And it is.

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:14.420
Seems pretty simple.

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:15.680
It kind of is.

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:17.620
And it seems fun, which it is.

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:23.500
But then you play it yourself and it feels like everyone is out to get you in this game.

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:25.920
Like you're talking about the balls being random.

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:36.660
But I kind of feel like there are certain opportunities on this map where your only choice is just to kind of like tempt fate and say, all right, well, I'm not going to move.

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:40.440
And if the ball comes at me, that's just bad luck.

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:57.800
You know, like I've got a – I don't have any many other choices because sometimes where you are on the pyramid, especially when you're higher up, it's hard to like quickly get around these things because there's more than three on the screen.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:00.540
You're running out of available squares to you.

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:02.640
And a lot of times you just get cut off.

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:14.900
And I feel like that was just a lack of enemy programming where – I'm not saying that the game needed to be planned of where balls dropped and where the snakes appear.

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:24.820
But there should have been some more rules about how enemies navigate the stage in a way that you're not necessarily cut off all the time.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:31.120
You know, like a lot of times you're just stuck to a zone and you're repeatedly touching the squares that you've already turned.

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:33.800
You're just touching them again and again to avoid obstacles.

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:40.360
And that's where things get unsatisfying because the fun part of this game is watching the colors change from yellow to green.

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:43.020
As crazy as that sounds, that is the fun part of this game.

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:52.020
And if I'm not doing that, if I'm only changing my direction and hoping that I don't forget how I control my D-pad, that's not a very fun game.

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:56.540
That's just me playing like 3D Frogger with Q-Bert.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:10.800
Yeah. One other thing that I'll say about this, just to compare it to Pac-Man again, is like – okay, so Pac-Man, there's four ghosts.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:15.120
I don't know if there's ever more than four enemies on a screen of Q-Bert.

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:17.060
But it always feels like there's more.

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:28.340
And I think it's because in Pac-Man, you – it's a very minimal – like just even in like color space, it's a very minimal look.

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:32.580
It's always a black background and all of the enemies are bright.

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:37.160
Whereas in Q-Bert, everything is different colors.

00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:40.520
Like every level has some new bright color.

00:22:41.660 --> 00:22:44.100
And your enemies just sort of blend in.

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:57.460
So like whereas with – when you're playing Pac-Man, you can just use your peripheral vision to sort of do your own bit of like crowd control and just to get an idea of everything.

00:22:57.460 --> 00:23:08.580
Whereas I just lose the enemies in the noise of the screen in this game coupled with other things that, again, I'll get into visually.

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:16.440
But it's just – it's very – it's very hard to make heads or tails of what's happening, I think, for me.

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:22.960
Because you also need to – you know, we can get into the visual thing I feel like because that's going to open up a lot of conversation.

00:23:23.180 --> 00:23:27.760
But you need to always keep track of just like what you're doing.

00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:38.180
You know, like this isn't as simple as a shmup where it's like you know kind of how you're controlling your spaceship and you're focusing more on all of the other enemies and working on dodging them.

00:23:38.200 --> 00:23:40.500
Because you're all on the same space.

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:46.720
You know, like it's just – if you imagine graph paper, like you could lay that over and see that all.

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:57.520
Q-Bert changes this like established way of looking at video games and instead you're focused more on like am I about to jump on the right space?

00:23:58.040 --> 00:24:00.680
You know? Am I about to do the right thing?

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:03.780
I don't care where the enemies are and then it's like all right, cool.

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:08.160
You did that thing but now surprise like you bumped into an enemy and you're dead.

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:18.900
Yeah. I mean I do like the idea of like having to think through your actions but I think everything else sort of that's acting against that experience.

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:21.940
I do like the idea in theory.

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:24.400
It just doesn't seem to gel.

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:36.900
And I think the number one thing, at least for me, that isn't exactly like the programming or just the game design itself is – and let me know if you felt this too.

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:57.620
I was getting some weird optical illusions where – because if you look at any board of Q-Bert, it's really just your brain telling you like, yes, this is a corner of a pyramid or something.

00:24:57.620 --> 00:25:03.200
And that's just like the orientation that you see.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:14.080
But I would keep getting like this effect where the whole thing would just sort of invert and now I'm looking up at a corner of a pyramid and Q-Bert looks really weird.

00:25:14.320 --> 00:25:19.420
And do you know what I'm talking about or am I just sort of saying words right now?

00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:21.460
I could totally understand.

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:26.460
Like you did paint a good picture there that like the perspective changed.

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:27.820
It did not for me.

00:25:27.960 --> 00:25:29.420
It didn't. Okay. It didn't.

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:32.620
But no, but I know exactly how Q-Bert looks.

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:41.600
And I think if you suffer from something like that, right, or even just suffering from like losing the plane that you're on, right?

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:44.420
Like there's that one enemy that comes from the sides.

00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:46.320
Yeah. That also throws you off.

00:25:46.460 --> 00:25:47.400
Yeah. That's part of it.

00:25:47.400 --> 00:25:49.240
It throws you off because now you're like, okay, wait a minute.

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:52.020
Like am I, you know, like which tile am I on?

00:25:52.120 --> 00:25:57.180
And I think that's probably not to the fault of the game, right?

00:25:57.300 --> 00:26:07.400
Like I think that's like player dependent, similar to how like, no, just similar to like how some people can't play, you know, games with flashing images because they'll have a seizure, right?

00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:10.900
Yeah, yeah. Some people just can't handle the perspective shift.

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:18.320
I get very dizzy playing VR games where I have to walk, you know, like I actually feel like I'm going to throw up if I have to actually walk.

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:20.560
So I like games that warp you from place to place.

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:22.160
This is definitely a thing that happens.

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:30.400
I don't know how you solve it, but I could see that would make the game so much more difficult if you constantly lose your, you know, your place value.

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:35.680
Yeah, if it just had any amount of parallax, I think it would be fine.

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:49.740
But because it's at this nominally isometric angle where like the only clues of what your perspective is, is just like what you like, you just sort of trained to think of it that way.

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:55.820
Yeah, it can, I don't know, I didn't feel sick, but I did get very disoriented.

00:26:56.820 --> 00:27:13.840
I imagine a level of Qbert where the cubes actually like shift up and down, you know, so like in the middle of your playing, you have to be cautious of the moving cubes as they go from like a top heavy pyramid to like a V shape, you know.

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:26.600
Now, that would be something that would definitely trip up me, but would also be worth checking out as an idea as well, like kind of a, you know, a Tetris effect version of Qbert.

00:27:27.080 --> 00:27:28.120
Yeah, that'd be interesting.

00:27:28.500 --> 00:27:38.160
So did you use those rainbow disc elevators at the sides of the levels or did those just taunt you as like, you know, you totally can use us, but you won't?

00:27:39.100 --> 00:27:43.060
I know, I feel like you're playing on hard mode if you don't.

00:27:43.840 --> 00:27:58.040
But I did get tricked a lot, again, via perspective of, and I know the rules of the game, this is all my fault, but I would jump in the wrong direction and just jump off the screen when I tried to get to it.

00:27:58.120 --> 00:28:02.960
I'd say like once every six times I tried to use it, I would just kill myself.

00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:12.860
So they were always like just the siren singing you to the rocks, but I did my best.

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:15.720
I like that they're there, don't get me wrong.

00:28:15.880 --> 00:28:23.260
I just think it's funny how it's something that to me is like an obvious, like, yes, like get out of jail free card, right?

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:27.160
Like I just need to get on the elevator and then I'll be back up at the top and I'll be away from all these enemies.

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:35.000
But it felt like whenever I was heading towards them, I was going to be bumped into some enemies or to something, some kind of trap.

00:28:35.520 --> 00:28:38.460
I did ride the elevator successfully a few times, so it felt nice.

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:40.480
And it's definitely nice that they have them.

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:43.880
I think the one-time use thing is an interesting decision.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:50.320
Pac-Man didn't like close the gates if you go from the left side to the right side, you know, if you like warp around.

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:57.100
Right. You know, like it probably didn't need to be a one-time use thing, but they did choose that.

00:28:57.220 --> 00:28:59.980
They did choose that and I don't know what the correct answer is there.

00:29:00.220 --> 00:29:01.860
Obviously, I'm not great at this game.

00:29:02.060 --> 00:29:05.020
I played it a bunch though because I really do enjoy the concept.

00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:10.680
Yeah. I just think – I think something about the NES mode, I don't know if it's just harder or something like that, but it was hard.

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:16.520
Yeah, I don't know because I can't fully remember my experience playing on a machine.

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:18.700
It cannot be that different.

00:29:19.960 --> 00:29:34.920
I know one thing that gets people tripped up in this game and you kind of get used to it is just that input delay because you do have to watch the hop, which is always like – it always takes the same amount of time.

00:29:35.880 --> 00:29:38.860
But I think to some people it could feel sticky.

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:49.700
Maybe the game feel on the arcade version feels a little different than on the NES.

00:29:50.680 --> 00:30:04.740
But no, I also found this game very hard to control between the perspective and the density of enemies and everything that we've mentioned so far.

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:19.440
To get back to the delay though, that is an interesting observation and was something that I liked about the game that not all the enemies and you moved on like a synchronized clock from space to space.

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:26.560
It wasn't like a board game in that respect where everybody moves at the same times and obviously that's true.

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:30.560
Like you could just stand on a particular space and everybody else still moves along.

00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:43.160
But there were weird moments where like you could just narrowly dodge an enemy that's technically like on the same platform as you but if you jump off like a beat early or like leave a beat later.

00:30:43.160 --> 00:31:05.460
There's leeway there and that was – that afforded me a lot more gameplay time than it cost me because there's kind of like an advantage to the brain there than to have that leeway where you don't have to be this like frame perfect of like, all right, well, if he's going up, I'm going down.

00:31:05.460 --> 00:31:07.060
Oh, wait, there's this other guy on the left.

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:08.220
He's going to hit me this way.

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:13.760
It would be interesting to see a – you know, we're just creating so many Q-Bert games tonight.

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:16.500
Are you talking about like a necrodancer Q-Bert right now?

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:20.900
Yeah, yeah. It would be interesting to see that now that I think about it.

00:31:21.220 --> 00:31:31.460
Yeah. But like I'm glad that this game had some looseness to the hitbox so that you could kind of escape some narrow moments.

00:31:31.460 --> 00:31:49.460
Yeah, I did appreciate that and I could tell that it was a – it was something that they were thinking about because I feel like the default would just be like once that sprite has decided to move, it is – like the game now considers it in that box and you're just automatically dead.

00:31:49.640 --> 00:31:55.640
But I did appreciate that they would actually give you that leeway.

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:58.160
You do move slightly faster, I think.

00:31:58.260 --> 00:32:10.160
Like if you're like trying to outrun the snake from getting to the – from the bottom of the screen to the top, you could easily beat him there and maybe give yourself some room to maneuver.

00:32:12.440 --> 00:32:20.300
But I think that there were enough instances where you were just painted into a corner that it didn't always help that much.

00:32:23.380 --> 00:32:27.320
And I talked about Q-Bert cursing at the start of this episode.

00:32:27.560 --> 00:32:30.180
But that's like a – that's a funny thing, right?

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:37.700
Like there's actually – there's something interesting and unique about that that caught my attention even like back in 2002 when I was playing this game as a kid.

00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:43.020
And I think, you know, it's not edgy or anything like that.

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:48.560
But it's just like – it's just a playful thing to have like a – you know, he could not be cursing for all we know.

00:32:48.660 --> 00:32:50.740
He could just – that could be the Q-Bertian language.

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:53.820
I'm sure that that's what the designers would say.

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:59.960
But I think that – I think he's actually saying like every four-letter word we can think of.

00:33:00.080 --> 00:33:01.580
I like to think of him that way.

00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:03.520
Sure. But like I don't know.

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:10.460
Did you feel like – for me that was like a significant addition to the character, you know?

00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:14.160
Like actually like a small little thing that went like a long way.

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:30.700
Yeah. He's actually a flawed being of some sort that gets frustrated with himself and I guess is just more somewhat annoyed with his enemies than actually thinking them as enemies.

00:33:30.700 --> 00:33:38.980
I think he could read into it and maybe everyone will have a different interpretation of Q-Bert's psyche, you know?

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:42.740
Yeah. It's just every time he dies, he's having a gamer moment.

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:46.540
An epic gamer moment.

00:33:47.180 --> 00:33:58.780
Exactly. Sean, would you be shocked if I told you that the original title for this game was at symbol, exclamation mark, hashtag, question mark, at symbol, exclamation mark?

00:33:58.780 --> 00:34:02.840
Mark? I would say that that's a really weird thing to make up.

00:34:02.960 --> 00:34:04.940
So I would be inclined to believe you.

00:34:05.620 --> 00:34:07.200
And I am telling the truth.

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:15.100
But I just think, you know, the most interesting thing is like this was like an actual title.

00:34:15.240 --> 00:34:16.300
Like it never shipped, I think.

00:34:16.360 --> 00:34:18.320
I don't think it ever shipped with that title.

00:34:18.440 --> 00:34:20.580
But they built it into the arcade cabinets first.

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:28.200
And it got like all the way to like the president of the company, which, you know, this is Godaleb.

00:34:28.540 --> 00:34:33.980
So they were like, I don't know if I'm saying that right, by the way, but they're a pinball company, not a video game developer.

00:34:34.160 --> 00:34:37.360
But like it got all the way up to the president and he was like, I kind of like it.

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:38.900
That's so weird.

00:34:39.160 --> 00:34:42.380
How would you even, what would you even refer to it as?

00:34:42.580 --> 00:34:44.200
Like static noise?

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:49.060
Yeah, you would say that game with the symbols, you know, like I think.

00:34:49.160 --> 00:34:50.340
Also, I think it's Gottlieb.

00:34:50.960 --> 00:34:53.880
Oh, okay. That's why they changed it though, right?

00:34:54.040 --> 00:34:58.440
Like there was just no marketable, you know, there's not marketable to call it that.

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:07.180
But I think it is really funny to think that like, you know, even back then people were kind of testing the limits of what people would put up with.

00:35:07.180 --> 00:35:14.680
You know, like the original title being just a string of symbols, you know, feels like something that we would now see on Adult Swim.

00:35:15.740 --> 00:35:16.780
Yeah, for sure.

00:35:17.980 --> 00:35:18.980
I'd say so.

00:35:19.380 --> 00:35:32.140
And Qbert was supposed to shoot, I guess, like a black ball out of his, you know, if you notice his orange round body has another like, you know, a part of it.

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:35.900
An orifice. Yeah, it's a, I guess a mouth, right?

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:39.600
Let's call it a mouth. But it is really more like a freaking cannon if you ask me.

00:35:39.860 --> 00:35:47.160
So I think he was going to shoot cannonballs out of these things as another way to take out the enemies.

00:35:47.160 --> 00:35:59.180
But Warren Davis, who was the creator of the game, he wanted to, you know, like remove as many complexities as possible and make it so that you could play the game with just one hand.

00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:07.140
So remove the ability to shoot, just made the game about stepping on the spaces, nothing more.

00:36:07.800 --> 00:36:09.280
Do you think you went in the right direction?

00:36:09.440 --> 00:36:13.340
Do you think shooting might have been another way to make this game a little more fun?

00:36:13.820 --> 00:36:18.880
No, I think the charm of it is, again, the simplicity that we're talking about.

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:23.300
I mean, it does turn into a very anxious simplicity.

00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:28.840
But in terms of that decision, I agree that that was the way to go.

00:36:29.020 --> 00:36:34.260
I don't think I would – it would add anything if I was – it was like even how would that work?

00:36:34.260 --> 00:36:40.220
Like you're going to just throw them – like it would be another obstacle, but it might hit the other dudes.

00:36:40.640 --> 00:36:42.120
I guess.

00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:45.560
I like it the way it is in comparison to that.

00:36:46.040 --> 00:36:51.240
I also like Q-Bert being a harmless guy in the wrong space.

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:55.080
You know, like he's innocent and you care for him and you don't want him to get hurt.

00:36:55.860 --> 00:36:58.500
And then when he does get hurt, he curses and it's cute, you know.

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:06.120
If he's shooting a gun and killing snakes, then when he curses, now he's like a badass.

00:37:06.140 --> 00:37:08.620
You know, it's like, oh, this guy is like Bart Simpson.

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:11.400
Is Bart Simpson a badass?

00:37:12.500 --> 00:37:19.900
Well, no, I just – I guess like I was trying to think of like what would be the Q-Bertian version of a – you know, forget it.

00:37:20.100 --> 00:37:24.480
It's not important. Also, Q-Bert predates Bart Simpson by six years.

00:37:24.660 --> 00:37:30.420
True. So I just think, you know, not the badass part, but maybe just like the, you know, kid with attitude.

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:34.440
Yeah, he's got Q-Bertitude.

00:37:35.200 --> 00:37:36.720
Yeah, he's actually Q-Bart.

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:39.260
Whoa. I said it.

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:40.680
Anything else you want to talk about?

00:37:42.620 --> 00:37:45.540
I don't – I really don't know.

00:37:45.860 --> 00:37:47.980
I feel like you have some other stuff.

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:54.140
I do. I have some sequels and spinoffs stuff because you know Q-Bert – the Q-Bert craze.

00:37:54.300 --> 00:37:56.080
The Q-Bert quays was real.

00:37:56.340 --> 00:38:03.500
The Q-Bert quays. And they did release a sequel game in the arcade called Q-Bert's Cubes.

00:38:03.820 --> 00:38:08.300
Now, I don't know if that was the right title for a sequel.

00:38:08.620 --> 00:38:13.620
You know, I don't think – I don't think Q-Bert 2 would have been any better, but it wouldn't have been any worse.

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:15.340
Q-Bert's Cubes.

00:38:15.420 --> 00:38:17.260
What do you think? Is that a good title?

00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:26.360
I think that it's just like because Q-Bert – like the Q part of Q-Bert is trying to make reference to cubes because you're hopping on cubes.

00:38:26.360 --> 00:38:31.860
And I think just to add cubes again to the end is just adding redundancies.

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:41.480
But if you want to be as flexible with the pronunciation of the Q as we have been, then maybe Q-Bert's Q-Bert's Q-Bert would have been better.

00:38:41.960 --> 00:38:46.760
Maybe that's actually how they intended people to – and you think about it, right?

00:38:46.760 --> 00:38:50.680
If it was a bunch of symbols, would the second game have just been more symbols?

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:57.400
Would it have been like at sign, exclamation mark, hashtag, question mark, at sign, exclamation mark, parentheses?

00:38:57.900 --> 00:38:59.460
I think it would be in parentheses.

00:38:59.500 --> 00:39:00.900
But yeah, we're on the same page.

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:02.320
Oh, okay. Yeah, that's actually good.

00:39:02.340 --> 00:39:06.220
I like that. So anyway, similar gameplay, Q-Bert's Cubes to Q-Bert.

00:39:06.440 --> 00:39:08.640
But now it's in three dimensions.

00:39:08.860 --> 00:39:10.020
Now, they describe it that way.

00:39:10.100 --> 00:39:11.760
I thought the first game was in three dimensions.

00:39:11.760 --> 00:39:13.760
I don't see how this is really any different.

00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:21.560
In fact, I would argue this one is less in three dimensions because the cubes are just kind of more spaced out now.

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:28.120
Like you can tell that they're all boxes and everything, but they're not actually like touching each other.

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:30.380
It seems very strange.

00:39:30.580 --> 00:39:38.880
I don't think – maybe I'm not looking at this the right way and maybe there was supposed to be played with some 3D glasses or something that made it that kind of 3D.

00:39:38.880 --> 00:39:45.780
But something about this just – it just seems wrong to me and I can understand why I've never heard about this game before today.

00:39:46.260 --> 00:39:59.220
Yeah, I'm watching it right now and yeah, it's just – they've just separated the surfaces and I guess changed the way – oh, I see how it is.

00:39:59.220 --> 00:40:01.880
So I get it.

00:40:02.180 --> 00:40:17.580
So basically it's as if you jumping on to the surfaces didn't just change the face color, but you actually had to rotate the entire cube so that the correct face was on top.

00:40:17.800 --> 00:40:19.580
Oh, it's a Rubik's Cube now.

00:40:19.580 --> 00:40:30.300
So if every Rubik's Cube had like just one color on each side and you couldn't change all of the little – basically, yeah.

00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:34.620
Okay, but also a Rubik's Cube with only one color, that's my kind of Rubik's Cube.

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:37.400
All right. Yeah.

00:40:38.020 --> 00:40:47.600
Now the next game that I'm going to talk about, I'm sure there are other ports of Q-Bert and it got on every Atari console, 2,600, 5,200, 7,600.

00:40:48.180 --> 00:40:49.080
They're all on there.

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:56.540
So I'm not going to talk about ports, but I have Q-Bert 3, which this game better be actually called Q-Bert Cubed.

00:40:57.580 --> 00:41:00.240
Okay, because like that is a mistake.

00:41:00.500 --> 00:41:06.000
If the 3 isn't supposed to be like for Cubed, I think that they really botched this game.

00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:08.200
It is not in the correct place.

00:41:08.280 --> 00:41:09.680
It's just Q-Bert 3.

00:41:10.360 --> 00:41:14.960
I can't believe that they would opt for Q-Bert 3 when Q-Bert Cubed is in front of them.

00:41:15.140 --> 00:41:17.520
Like it's literally staring at them.

00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:21.020
They just had to move the 3 up a little bit more.

00:41:21.960 --> 00:41:23.560
Incredible. Instead we get Q-Bert 3.

00:41:23.700 --> 00:41:24.700
Where was Q-Bert 2?

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:37.080
I know it was Q-Bert Cubes, but I can't think of a series that goes like the game with a subtitle and then the game 3.

00:41:37.380 --> 00:41:38.700
Like I just can't think of that.

00:41:40.800 --> 00:41:44.900
Man, I'm sure there's something I could think of because I know that that still happens.

00:41:45.160 --> 00:41:53.880
But I'm looking at footage now of Q-Bert 3 and it's even more just busy.

00:41:53.880 --> 00:41:58.440
Like I don't know how anyone can focus playing this game.

00:41:58.500 --> 00:42:02.520
Well, this is Q-Bert with new layouts and new patterns.

00:42:02.960 --> 00:42:07.620
So for instance, you can – you know, like the boxes are now American flags.

00:42:07.860 --> 00:42:09.320
Yeah. No, I see that.

00:42:09.460 --> 00:42:19.420
But like you've got this psychedelic background that keeps changing and I think this is the game that's going to give – like forget about modern video games.

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:25.580
Like this game with the flashing psychedelic imagery in the background, that's what's going to give you a seizure.

00:42:26.040 --> 00:42:32.280
Yeah. And you know, it's really interesting because I did play what I'm going to officially call Q-Bert Cubed and own the rights to.

00:42:32.980 --> 00:42:43.460
When I played this game, before you die, but as you're about to die, like let's say you're jumping off the – you know, and they know that you're not going to land on another platform.

00:42:43.600 --> 00:42:45.320
Like you're jumping off into space, right?

00:42:45.460 --> 00:42:54.320
Yeah. Before you even get close to dying, just by the fact that you press the input, everything else on the screen stops.

00:42:54.560 --> 00:42:56.220
And that just feels weird to me.

00:42:56.360 --> 00:43:04.720
You know, like that – there's something both glitchy and uncomfortable about like every enemy just like halting in space.

00:43:05.100 --> 00:43:11.020
You know, like everything just freezing up because you've pressed a button that is eventually going to kill you.

00:43:11.100 --> 00:43:14.180
But the second you press it, everything is off limits.

00:43:14.180 --> 00:43:16.100
It's like you cannot undo this decision.

00:43:16.340 --> 00:43:17.940
Now you need to watch what you've done.

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:29.320
I think it's more a comment on the fact that in this universe – like this entire universe is just a projection of Q-Bert's body.

00:43:29.460 --> 00:43:34.400
Like it's a solipsistic game where the only real thing is Q-Bert.

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:39.580
And now that Q-Bert is dead, everything else ceases to be.

00:43:39.920 --> 00:43:42.920
I see. I thought of it more like a stage play.

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:44.480
You know, like everyone is just acting.

00:43:44.720 --> 00:43:49.200
And then when Q-Bert jumps off stage and kills himself, all the other actors are like, oh, shit.

00:43:49.320 --> 00:43:50.820
Like is that supposed to happen?

00:43:50.960 --> 00:43:52.120
Like it wasn't in the script.

00:43:53.100 --> 00:43:55.760
Okay. That's not like a stage play then.

00:43:55.760 --> 00:43:58.680
That's more like a stage play gone wrong.

00:43:59.280 --> 00:44:00.200
Right, right, right. Sorry.

00:44:00.240 --> 00:44:01.940
I didn't know I needed the extra subtitle.

00:44:02.060 --> 00:44:04.980
I'll be sure to make that the second game will be called that.

00:44:04.980 --> 00:44:06.980
And then the third game will just be called stage play three.

00:44:10.020 --> 00:44:14.940
Anyway, Q-Bert for Sega Dreamcast is up next.

00:44:15.260 --> 00:44:18.620
Okay. It also came to like PlayStation 1 and stuff.

00:44:18.780 --> 00:44:20.260
So I don't know why I singled that out.

00:44:20.320 --> 00:44:24.660
I just think it's funny when like, you know, consoles that people don't like.

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:27.980
I don't know. I never thought there was a Q-Bert game on Sega Dreamcast.

00:44:28.140 --> 00:44:32.000
But I'm sure like people who collect Sega Dreamcast own this copy of Q-Bert.

00:44:32.900 --> 00:44:34.720
They don't give it a subtitle or anything.

00:44:34.860 --> 00:44:36.320
It's just Q-Bert all over again.

00:44:36.320 --> 00:44:47.360
And cute, it's actually like, it's like Donkey Kong 94 in the sense that you start off playing the normal Q-Bert arcade game like with the old graphics and everything.

00:44:47.620 --> 00:44:54.120
And then you move into the 3D puzzle adventure game that Q-Bert on Sega Dreamcast is.

00:44:54.420 --> 00:44:55.660
Whoa. Okay.

00:44:56.220 --> 00:44:57.880
I like that.

00:44:58.300 --> 00:45:00.780
I'm not seeing it right now.

00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:04.520
Because I am, again, I'm watching it.

00:45:04.520 --> 00:45:07.640
It's just like, it looks like an N64 game.

00:45:07.840 --> 00:45:08.900
I don't know. Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:45:08.920 --> 00:45:10.120
Make no mistake about it.

00:45:10.180 --> 00:45:11.000
It ain't pretty.

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:16.780
But they, I think that this game was always going to come too late.

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:22.940
Like even on N64, it was going to be, it was going to be weird that it was there.

00:45:23.300 --> 00:45:25.340
Yeah. Just untextured.

00:45:26.020 --> 00:45:27.200
Yeah. He was already 3D.

00:45:27.400 --> 00:45:29.540
It was already a 3D arcade game in 1983.

00:45:30.060 --> 00:45:36.640
Now when you embrace the actual bad graphics of like an N64, like that's not progress.

00:45:36.660 --> 00:45:38.020
That's actually a step backwards.

00:45:38.180 --> 00:45:41.400
I think about this with Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles.

00:45:42.380 --> 00:45:46.000
The arcade games look awesome for their sprite work and everything.

00:45:46.260 --> 00:45:48.140
And then they like remastered some of those games.

00:45:48.260 --> 00:45:52.060
Like they did Turtles in Time HD on Xbox 360.

00:45:52.680 --> 00:45:57.660
And it was just like crappy 3D models, but the same exact game recreated.

00:45:57.840 --> 00:46:01.840
And it's like, who were the fans of the beautiful sprite work arcade game that said,

00:46:01.940 --> 00:46:06.760
just give me the exact same thing again, but with Xbox 360 graphics.

00:46:06.940 --> 00:46:08.520
Like I don't think any of them said that.

00:46:08.620 --> 00:46:15.500
It's like the, like the Final Fantasy 6 or 3, whatever you want to say for the phones

00:46:15.500 --> 00:46:19.060
and how they just have like chibi 3D stuff now.

00:46:19.060 --> 00:46:21.620
And I, I didn't want that.

00:46:22.080 --> 00:46:22.960
Nobody wants that.

00:46:22.960 --> 00:46:26.840
So I hear you. And they did that for every Final Fantasy game that was part of the older lines, right?

00:46:26.900 --> 00:46:31.340
Like I think even you play Final Fantasy 2 and 3, which were Japan exclusives.

00:46:31.520 --> 00:46:35.040
You can play them on mobile, but you have to play them with the chibi version.

00:46:35.180 --> 00:46:37.980
Like you can't just say, give me the, give me the NES art.

00:46:38.680 --> 00:46:39.480
Demand satisfaction.

00:46:40.520 --> 00:46:43.460
Yep. Last on my list is Qbert rebooted.

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:52.840
I don't know. Felt like there could have been another cube thing there, like Qbert re, re cubed or Qbert re, re cubed.

00:46:52.840 --> 00:46:54.140
No, re cubed. Re cubed.

00:46:54.780 --> 00:46:56.100
Yeah. I like it.

00:46:56.180 --> 00:47:01.980
Re cubed. Well, it looks like here he's actually not jumping on cubes all the time anymore.

00:47:02.140 --> 00:47:04.700
Like they can be like hexagonal.

00:47:05.080 --> 00:47:06.820
Yeah. They're hexagons this time.

00:47:07.020 --> 00:47:11.940
So now you have six points of direction and this is a mobile game.

00:47:11.940 --> 00:47:14.420
So I imagine it's like swiping on your phone.

00:47:14.780 --> 00:47:15.880
Oh yeah. That makes sense.

00:47:16.180 --> 00:47:17.300
In those directions or something.

00:47:17.540 --> 00:47:22.360
I couldn't play this one because, you know, I mean, maybe we have the technology, but I

00:47:22.360 --> 00:47:24.140
don't know how to emulate mobile games.

00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:26.180
Well, it says it's on PS4.

00:47:27.360 --> 00:47:28.260
PS4? Yeah.

00:47:29.040 --> 00:47:29.840
Qbert rebooted.

00:47:30.220 --> 00:47:31.860
PS4. A game that looks like that.

00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:32.960
Yeah. Went to the PS4.

00:47:33.280 --> 00:47:34.700
Absolutely. Wow.

00:47:34.920 --> 00:47:36.080
Okay. I mean, why not?

00:47:36.160 --> 00:47:41.720
Why not? But it's, so then it had to have also just been like, you know, using, uh, you know,

00:47:42.020 --> 00:47:43.120
an analog stick. Yeah.

00:47:43.360 --> 00:47:48.440
Right. Yeah. Uh, but the six points of direction instead of the classic four, I'm for that.

00:47:48.600 --> 00:47:53.960
I think that that out of all the pitches tonight is the one that makes sense that it

00:47:53.960 --> 00:47:58.740
was greenlit. You know, we pitched a lot of different Qbert games, but I think when you say, well, what

00:47:58.740 --> 00:47:59.820
if we just take the same game?

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:01.620
What if Polygon, but more?

00:48:02.320 --> 00:48:03.200
Right. Exactly.

00:48:03.520 --> 00:48:07.060
I feel like this is the one executives would be like, of course.

00:48:07.220 --> 00:48:10.440
And so obviously it was made back in 2014 though.

00:48:10.680 --> 00:48:14.660
So for PS4 though, so maybe go spend, I know it's not 60 bucks, but I'm going to make the

00:48:14.660 --> 00:48:17.260
joke. Go spend 60 bucks on Qbert rebooted.

00:48:17.460 --> 00:48:19.520
Yeah. Nintendo would do it.

00:48:19.600 --> 00:48:21.080
They would charge you 60 bucks for that game.

00:48:21.080 --> 00:48:22.900
Oh, they would. On Switch.

00:48:25.960 --> 00:48:31.460
But we don't charge, uh, anything for, um, the most important part of our show, Sean.

00:48:31.460 --> 00:48:36.940
Uh, we don't, we don't like ad support the essential games list, which is the part I'm

00:48:36.940 --> 00:48:40.220
talking about here. No, it's the Nabisco essential games list.

00:48:40.500 --> 00:48:41.300
Right, right, right.

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:45.900
This is, this is free to the listeners, power to the players.

00:48:46.180 --> 00:48:48.160
This is something we do every episode.

00:48:48.320 --> 00:48:53.040
And somehow we've been talking about Qbert for a really long time, but we also need to,

00:48:53.120 --> 00:48:54.520
we need to wrap up our time.

00:48:54.620 --> 00:48:59.280
We need to come to a decision and we're going to make that decision on the essential games

00:48:59.280 --> 00:49:12.060
list. I would say that on the inaugural, uh, Nestle essential games list, I would say that Qbert

00:49:12.060 --> 00:49:19.420
is not essential just, it, just because like it, every other arcade game that like nothing's

00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:20.920
really changed from the arcade.

00:49:20.920 --> 00:49:28.600
Uh, I, I can't put a game like that on the essential games list and, uh, I can hardly put

00:49:28.600 --> 00:49:31.020
Qbert on any surface without dying.

00:49:31.340 --> 00:49:32.400
So that's me.

00:49:32.400 --> 00:49:33.420
I like that.

00:49:33.420 --> 00:49:34.240
I like that ending.

00:49:34.440 --> 00:49:37.800
That was very nice. And, um, you know, shout out to Nabisco.

00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:40.520
What can I say? I'm, I'm full, I'm all in on it now.

00:49:40.680 --> 00:49:41.660
I believe in them.

00:49:42.120 --> 00:49:43.400
That's how sponsorships work.

00:49:43.420 --> 00:49:45.620
If you just say them enough, they sponsor you.

00:49:45.740 --> 00:49:48.560
They're like, oh, you know, these guys are talking about us an awful lot.

00:49:49.360 --> 00:49:56.020
Um, you know, here's where I fall with Qbert, uh, because I fall off the stage all the time

00:49:56.020 --> 00:50:04.520
with Qbert. There's something about like a game like this being fun for someone like me, who's awful

00:50:04.520 --> 00:50:09.960
at it. Like that, that, that takes a lot to make a game that you can still enjoy no matter how

00:50:09.960 --> 00:50:10.820
bad you are at it.

00:50:10.900 --> 00:50:12.540
Right. How many games do you know like that?

00:50:12.700 --> 00:50:16.760
So Qbert does have something going for it, but I really think that's all it has going for

00:50:16.760 --> 00:50:25.560
it. It's kind of understandable to me why the Qbert Quays died in 1983 with the video game crash.

00:50:26.580 --> 00:50:31.420
I came up with a lot of ideas for how to make Qbert a little better, but even those ideas

00:50:32.040 --> 00:50:33.660
aren't going to get it very far.

00:50:34.080 --> 00:50:37.720
Um, into like, you know, my, my like, oh, now I should play that game.

00:50:37.760 --> 00:50:39.540
Like, I don't even think I would, I think I'd skip them all.

00:50:40.140 --> 00:50:45.960
So Qbert is one of those games that you find in a barcade or, you know, on a trip and you're

00:50:45.960 --> 00:50:48.080
just like, oh, this guy has a Qbert in his deli.

00:50:48.240 --> 00:50:49.320
Okay, cool. Let's play it.

00:50:49.480 --> 00:50:53.660
Like those things will happen to you in life because they sold a lot of Qbert cabinets.

00:50:53.800 --> 00:50:55.520
Yeah. And that's when you play Qbert.

00:50:55.520 --> 00:50:59.980
You don't play Qbert when you're looking through a list of essential games that were

00:50:59.980 --> 00:51:01.700
released on the Nintendo Entertainment System.

00:51:01.980 --> 00:51:08.420
In fact, I'd go as far as to say you don't actively seek out a Qbert arcade cabinet.

00:51:08.600 --> 00:51:09.640
Qbert will find you.

00:51:10.240 --> 00:51:11.200
Yeah. You know what I'm saying?

00:51:11.300 --> 00:51:15.740
Like you don't, you don't like when you're building a arcade for your downstairs basement,

00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:17.840
right? Which like, I'm definitely not.

00:51:17.980 --> 00:51:19.960
So this is not, nobody's showing off here.

00:51:19.960 --> 00:51:25.420
If you're one of those people, you're only buying the Qbert arcade cabinet if you like

00:51:25.420 --> 00:51:27.160
were there in 1983.

00:51:27.240 --> 00:51:32.860
No one is saying I need to include Qbert in my list of arcade cabinets that I want to buy

00:51:32.860 --> 00:51:35.040
when the prices are like $600.

00:51:35.620 --> 00:51:37.080
You know, like no one's doing that.

00:51:37.080 --> 00:51:39.420
So that's how Qbert fleshes out here.

00:51:39.600 --> 00:51:45.400
It's not as tough a decision for me as some of our other classic arcade games because those

00:51:45.400 --> 00:51:50.660
are just great games that happen to also, you know, be on the NES and probably be slightly

00:51:50.660 --> 00:51:53.400
inferior. Qbert doesn't have that problem.

00:51:53.540 --> 00:51:56.960
Qbert is just, it's always been an inferior arcade game to the greats.

00:51:56.960 --> 00:52:00.520
Oh man, that's the, that was rough.

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:06.620
Yeah, I think you could say that I just spiked Qbert there.

00:52:06.900 --> 00:52:12.680
And the reason I use the word spiked is because we will be playing Tecmo Bowl next week.

00:52:12.940 --> 00:52:17.840
Hell yeah. Next week, Tecmo Bowl, everybody must play as the Bills.

